# Architecture Review Skill

Evidence-based architecture and design-quality review. When the user asks to review, audit, or critique a codebase's architecture, design, or structure — or asks "is this designed well", "is this overengineered", "is my folder structure right", "why is this so hard to change" — follow `skill/architecture-review/SKILL.md`.

Not for finding bugs or security vulnerabilities.

## Path resolution

All `references/...` paths resolve relative to **`skill/architecture-review/`**, not to the reviewed project. Review artifacts are written to `.architecture-review/` in the reviewed project root. Do not confuse the two trees.

## Loading order

1. `skill/architecture-review/SKILL.md` — modes, phases, domain router
2. `skill/architecture-review/references/rules.md` — evidence ledger, confidence tiers, Cost Gate, severity, finding schema, noise control, anti-fabrication. Load once; applies to all phases.
3. `references/phases/phase-N-*.md` — one at a time, as you reach each phase. Never pre-load.
4. `references/domains/*.md` — only the guides matching the shape identified in Phase 0. Release after Phase 3.
5. `references/tooling/*.md` — as needed in Phase 1, then release.

## Modes

- `quick` — phases 0, 1, 2, 7 (structural triage)
- `standard` — phases 0–7 (default)
- `deep` — phases 0–7, wider evidence sweep and more change scenarios
- `pr` — phases 0 (light), 2, 3, 5, 7, scoped to a diff
- `diff` — re-review; read `references/differential-protocol.md`
- `refactor` - apply one finding from a prior run after approval; read `references/refactor-protocol.md`

## Phases

0 recon & intent · 1 evidence harvest · 2 structure · 3 abstraction · 4 change-cost · 5 adversarial · 6 plan · 7 report

## Rules

1. Read-only — never modify a file in the reviewed project, never install into it; only `.architecture-review/` is written. No review phase fixes anything; `refactor` mode is a separate session.
2. Measure before you opine — Phase 1 runs tools and forms no judgments.
3. Never conclude "decoupled" from import edges alone — static graphs miss event buses, shared tables, and string-keyed registries. Phase 2 mandates an implicit-coupling census.
4. Every finding passes the 5-Point Cost Gate, `references/rules.md` §4. No demonstrated cost, no finding.
5. History before hypotheticals — a projected change is admissible only after a git search found no comparable past one, and only with a named anchor. Which commits qualify is defined in §4.
6. Every finding carries a tagged evidence ledger (§2); at least one `[read]` line; `[infer]`-only is discarded, not downgraded.
7. Confidence is derived from the ledger, not judged (§3) — ceilings C1–C5, lowest wins.
8. Never state a metric you did not compute or cite a line you did not open (§8). No scores, grades, or indices.
9. A clean review is a valid outcome. Manufacturing findings is the worst failure mode.

## Where the rules live

Confidence tiers, the Cost Gate, the evidence ledger, the severity/priority model, the finding schema, and the do-not-report list live in `skill/architecture-review/references/rules.md` §1–§9. **Load it once. Do not restate, summarize, or re-derive them.**
